/-!
# Buchsbaum--Eisenbud regular sequence from an exact free complex

Source: literature/stacks-project/algebra.tex, Proposition Tag 00N1 (blueprint:
lem-weil-pbc-exact-free-complex-last-minors-regular-sequence).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

/-- Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Proposition 00N1 (`proposition-what-exact`),
implication (1) => (2) at `i = e`; maximal minors as in Definition 00MV (`definition-rank`).
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-exact-free-complex-last-minors-regular-sequence)

An exact finite free complex over a Noetherian local ring has either unit last-minor
ideal or a regular sequence of length `e` in that ideal. The map
`φ_i : R^{n_i} → R^{n_{i-1}}` (`1 ≤ i ≤ e`) is given by its matrix `φ i`, acting by
`Matrix.mulVec`; the values of `n` and `φ` at indices outside the complex are unused.
The ideal `I` is generated by the determinants of the submatrices of `φ e` on `n_e`
rows listed in increasing order and all columns (empty minor `1` when `n_e = 0`).

Proof, following the six steps of the node:
1. Induct on `e ≥ 1`. Determinants commute with ring maps, so the last-minor ideal
   commutes with base change. If the last rank is zero, the empty minor is one.
2. Localize at each associated prime. Localization preserves the injection, the local
   ring is Noetherian of depth zero, and the injective free map has a unit maximal minor
   (`lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian`,
   `lem-weil-pbc-associated-prime-localization-depth-zero`,
   `lem-weil-pbc-localization-module-exact`,
   `lem-weil-pbc-depth-zero-injective-free-map-unit-minor`). Thus `I` avoids every
   associated prime.
3. If `I` is proper it lies in the maximal ideal. Finiteness of associated primes,
   prime avoidance, and the description of zero divisors give a nonzerodivisor `x ∈ I`
   (`lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-associated-primes-finite`,
   `lem-weil-pbc-prime-avoidance`,
   `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-zerodivisors-union-associated-primes`).
4. For `e = 1`, the sequence `[x]` is regular: multiplication is injective and the
   quotient is nonzero since `x` lies in the maximal ideal.
5. For `e ≥ 2`, reduce modulo `x`. The quotient is Noetherian local
   (`lem-weil-pbc-surjective-image-noetherian`, `lem-weil-pbc-quotient-local-structure`).
   The shortened complex remains exact (`lem-weil-pbc-free-complex-mod-nonzerodivisor-exact`).
   Its minor ideal is `I/xR`, which is proper. Induction gives a regular sequence of
   length `e - 1` there; choose lifts in `I`.
6. Prepend `x` to those lifts. Successive quotient identifications preserve injectivity
   of multiplication, and the final quotient is nonzero. This gives the required regular
   sequence of length `e` (`def-weil-pbc-regular-sequence`). -/
theorem exact_free_complex_last_minors_regular_sequence
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (e : ℕ) (he : 1 ≤ e) (n : ℕ → ℕ)
    (φ : ∀ i : ℕ, Matrix (Fin (n (i - 1))) (Fin (n i)) R)
    (hcomplex : ∀ i : ℕ, 1 ≤ i → i + 1 ≤ e →
      ∀ v : Fin (n (i + 1)) → R, (φ i).mulVec ((φ (i + 1)).mulVec v) = 0)
    (hexact : ∀ i : ℕ, 1 ≤ i → i + 1 ≤ e →
      Function.Exact (φ (i + 1)).mulVec (φ i).mulVec)
    (hinjective : Function.Injective (φ e).mulVec) :
    let I : Ideal R := Ideal.span {d : R | ∃ r : Fin (n e) → Fin (n (e - 1)),
      StrictMono r ∧ d = ((φ e).submatrix r id).det}
    I = ⊤ ∨ ∃ rs : List R, rs.length = e ∧
      (∀ x ∈ rs, x ∈ I) ∧ RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular R rs := by
  sorry
